<p>I never use many of these symbols because they’re too easy to confuse with Latin letters that look the same; this is especially true for the capitals. But once I got started, I just did the whole alphabet anyway. Maybe I’ll delete some of them as time goes on.</p>
<p>The set is defined with a double comma as the common prefix, and the abbreviations expand immediately—no waiting for a word break character.</p>
<p><img alt="Greek letters group information" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261116-Greek%20letters%20group%20information.png" title="Greek letters group information" width="80%"/></p>
<p>You may be wondering why I use <code>,,y</code> as the abbreviation for η. When I need to deal with a rotated coordinate system, ξ and η are typically the symbols I use for the rotated x and y coordinates, so it made sense (to me, anyway) for η to be <code>,,y</code>.</p>
<p><img alt="Coordinate systems" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261116-Coordinate%20systems.png" title="Coordinate systems" width="80%"/></p>
